1. Negation — the word 'not' barely moves the vector

   0.878  The supplier may raise prices.
          vs The supplier may not raise prices.
   0.727  Goods conform to the specification.
          vs Goods do not conform to the specification.
   1.000  The buyer may terminate for convenience.
          (identical) The buyer may terminate for convenience.
   A sentence and its exact opposite sit almost on top of each other.

2. Identifiers — every product code looks like every other product code

              001      004      011      023
   CLE-001    1.000    0.972    0.826    0.793
   CLE-004    0.972    1.000    0.815    0.794
   SAF-011    0.826    0.815    1.000    0.817
   PAC-023    0.793    0.794    0.817    1.000
   Asking for MRD-CLE-001 will happily return MRD-CLE-004.

3. Numbers — magnitude is not encoded

   1.000  a payment term of 30 days
   0.855  a payment term of 45 days
   0.861  a payment term of 300 days
   0.856  a payment term of 30 years
   '30 days' is closer to '30 years' than to '45 days'.

4. And the one that matters most: similarity is not confidence

   0.498  the right answer       The Supplier may adjust prices by no more th
   0.211  a related clause       The Supplier may terminate this Agreement on
   0.108  a different document   The Columbus depot will close for inventory 
   0.030  complete nonsense      Bananas are typically harvested while still 

The ordering is right, and that is all it is: an ordering.

Look at the top score. The correct, directly responsive answer scores 0.5 — not
0.9, not anything you would recognise as 'confident'. There is no threshold you
could set here that means 'good enough'. Set it at 0.4 and this passes; ask a
question with no answer in the corpus and the best of a hundred irrelevant
chunks will also clear 0.4, because something always ranks first.

A similarity score ranks candidates against each other. It says nothing about
whether the winner is any good. Chapter 13 needs a different signal for that,
and this is why.
